(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["me-easy-2026-009"]={"v":1,"id":"me-easy-2026-009","title":"ESE 2026 DEMO TEST ME","sourceTitle":"ESE 2026 DEMO TEST ME","branch":"MECHANICAL ENGINEERING","code":"ME","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Mechanical Engineering","seriesId":"me-easy-2026","num":9,"category":"demo","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Mechanical Engineering • Questions: 150","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Mechanical Engineering"},"source":"MECHANICAL ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Mechanical_Engineering/017_ESE 2026 DEMO TEST ME.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following is the correct relation for the tilt factor of diffuse radiation?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/3c/3c10dd449a18b5a2cd8f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>where ‘β’ is the slope of inclined surface</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>At equinox days, the declination angle is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"+23.45°"},{"key":"B","html":"–23.45°"},{"key":"C","html":"0°"},{"key":"D","html":"46.9°"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe declination angle changes from a maximum value of +23.45&deg; on June 21 to a minimum<br />\nof &ndash;23.45&deg; on December 22. The declination is zero on two equinox days i.e. March 22 and<br />\nSeptember 22.</p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>During winter, the optimum tilt angle of collectors is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/b4/b49357d1b9890b18ae82.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nDuring winter, the tilt angle is kept 10 - 15&deg; more than the latitude of the location while in<br />\nsummer it should be 10 - 15&deg; less than the latitude.</p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A cylindrical parabolic concentrator has 2.5 m width and 10 m length. The outside diameter of the absorber tube is 5 cm. What is the concentration ratio of the collector?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"11.9"},{"key":"B","html":"7.6"},{"key":"C","html":"15.6"},{"key":"D","html":"12.8"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/ca2623f2517923ec9182.png\" /></div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a solar pond, the bottom layer attains a maximum steady-state temperature of</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"60°C - 85°C"},{"key":"B","html":"150°C - 200°C"},{"key":"C","html":"300°C - 350°C"},{"key":"D","html":"20°C - 50°C"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. The highest value of power coefficient can be obtained with the propeller type rotors.<br />\n2. Multiblade and savonious type rotors have a high value of tip speed ratio varying from 3 to 7.<br />\n3. Propeller and Darrieus type rotors have a low value of tip speed ratio around unity.<br />\n4. Propeller rotor curves are not sharp while for other rotors the rise and fall of power coefficient is quite fast around the maximum value.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 4"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2 and 4"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nMultiblade and savonius type rotors have low value of tip speed ratio (&lambda;) around unity while the<br />\npropeller and Darrieus type rotors have a high value of &lambda; varying from 3 to 7. As the tip speed<br />\nratio increases, the number of blades decreases.</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A simple single-basin type tidal power plant has a basin area of 20 km<sup>2</sup>. The tide has a range of 10 m. The turbine stops operation when the head on it falls below 2 m. If the specific gravity of sea water is 1.025, the average power generated during one filling or emptying process is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"630.5 MW"},{"key":"B","html":"850.7 MW"},{"key":"C","html":"431.9 MW"},{"key":"D","html":"340.2 MW"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/be3c3e2c064c2ebc1128.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following statement is NOT correct regarding tidal power?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Tidal power is predictable"},{"key":"B","html":"It is free from pollution"},{"key":"C","html":"Tidal power plant output varies with the variation in tidal range"},{"key":"D","html":"Tidal power supply is continuous"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A progressive sea wave has a wave width of 100 m with a period of 10 seconds. The wave area is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3900 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"4500 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"12525 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"15600 m<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d9/d9e5d16c48894c08e3e1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In gasifiers, the producer gas is formed in</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Drying zone"},{"key":"B","html":"Pyrolysis zone"},{"key":"C","html":"Oxidation zone"},{"key":"D","html":"Reduction zone"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the favourable ratio of carbon to nitrogen for biogas production?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"15 : 1"},{"key":"B","html":"10 : 1"},{"key":"C","html":"30 : 1"},{"key":"D","html":"20 : 1"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nCarbon is required for energy while nitrogen for building cell protein. The consumption of carbon<br />\nis 30 to 35 times faster than that of nitrogen. A favourable ratio of C : N can be taken as 30 : 1. Any<br />\ndeviation from this ratio lowers the biogas production.</p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following is NOT correct for piezoelectric accelerometers?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Small size and a small weight"},{"key":"B","html":"High output impedance"},{"key":"C","html":"High sensitivity"},{"key":"D","html":"Low frequency response"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nHigh frequency response (10 Hz to 50 kHz). Unsuitable for applications where the input frequency<br />\nis lower than 10 Hz.</p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding applications of hall effect transducers:<br />\n1. Displacement measurement<br />\n2. Current measurement<br />\n3. Magnetic flux measurement<br />\n4. Fluid level measurement<br />\nWhich of the above applications are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A resistance wire strain guage has a guage factor of 5. Neglecting the piezo-resistive effect, the<br />\npoisson’s ratio will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1.6"},{"key":"B","html":"0.8"},{"key":"C","html":"2"},{"key":"D","html":"0.9"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/0197f20f8ff22f55b118.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding sensor characteristics:<br />\n1. Range is the difference between the largest and the smallest reading.<br />\n2. Span represents the algebric difference between the upper and lower range values of the sensor.<br />\n3. The ratio of the magnitude of input signal to output signal is called sensitivity.<br />\n4. The maximum differences in output at any measured value within the specified range when<br />\napproaching the point first with increasing and then with decreasing input is known as Hysteresis.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"},{"key":"B","html":"3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nSensitivity : The ratio of the magnitude of output signal to the input signal or response of<br />\nmeasuring system to the quantity being measured is called sensitivity. It is represented by the<br />\nslope of the calibration curve if the ordinates are expressed in actual units.</p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A load cell is essentially a</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Strain guage"},{"key":"B","html":"Thermistor"},{"key":"C","html":"Resistive potentiometer"},{"key":"D","html":"Inductive transducer"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nLoad cells are elastic devices that can be used for measurement of force through indirect methods.<br />\nWhen the combination of the strain guage-elastic member is used for weighing, it is called a &ldquo;load<br />\ncell&rdquo;.</p>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A two-port resistive network, used to reduce the signal level by a given amount is known as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Filter"},{"key":"B","html":"Amplifier"},{"key":"C","html":"Attenuator"},{"key":"D","html":"Transducer"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIn a number of applications, it is necessary to introduce a specified loss between the source and a matched load without altering the impedance relationship. Attenuators can be used for this purpose. An attenuator is a two-port resistive network and is used to reduce the signal level by a given amount.</p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Quantizing is defined as the transformation of a continuous digital output into a set of discrete input states.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Coding is assignment of a digital code word or number to each output state.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nQuantizing is defined as the transformation of a continuous analog input into a set of discrete output states.</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> An amplifier is a device which is used to increase or augment the weak signal.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Multiplexer is essentially an electronic switching device which enables each of the inputs to be sampled in turn.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding stepper motor:<br />\n1. It can rotate in both directions.<br />\n2. It can move in precise angular increments.<br />\n3. It can sustain a holding torque at zero speed.<br />\n4. It can be controlled with digital circuits.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In control modes, the rate of change of the output of the control is proportional to the input error signal in</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Two step mode"},{"key":"B","html":"Proportional mode"},{"key":"C","html":"Derivative mode"},{"key":"D","html":"Integral mode"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7d/7dcc4966e56f2f3c2132.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/cce126de532ac0d4d668.png\" /></div>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Two parallel plates kept at 0.02 m apart have a laminar flow of oil between them. The dynamic viscosity of oil is 0.8 poise and the upper plate moves horizontally at relative velocity of 0.5 m/s with respect to the lower plate, which is stationary. The pressure drops in the flow direction from 150 kPa to 100 kPa over a distance of 100 m.</p>\n\n<p>What is the velocity of oil at a distance of 0.01 m from the lower plate?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.027 m/s"},{"key":"B","html":"1.2 m/s"},{"key":"C","html":"0.56 m/s"},{"key":"D","html":"0.15 m/s"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a3/a334394eb080bfc23196.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Two parallel plates kept at 0.02 m apart have a laminar flow of oil between them. The dynamic viscosity of oil is 0.8 poise and the upper plate moves horizontally at relative velocity of 0.5 m/s with respect to the lower plate, which is stationary. The pressure drops in the flow direction from 150 kPa to 100 kPa over a distance of 100 m.</p>\n\n<p>What is the magnitude of discharge per unit width?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2.5 × 10<sup>–3</sup> m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"B","html":"15.18 × 10<sup>–4</sup> m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"C","html":"12.7 × 10<sup>–5</sup> m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"D","html":"9.16 × 10<sup>–3</sup> m<sup>3</sup>/s"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/507da68a09a5da5c3b4a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Two parallel plates kept at 0.02 m apart have a laminar flow of oil between them. The dynamic viscosity of oil is 0.8 poise and the upper plate moves horizontally at relative velocity of 0.5 m/s with respect to the lower plate, which is stationary. The pressure drops in the flow direction from 150 kPa to 100 kPa over a distance of 100 m.</p>\n\n<p>What is the value of shear stress at the centreline of plates?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1.2 N/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"2 N/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"3.2 N/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"5 N/m<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/53a871afb53e6d0cb152.png\" /></div>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A rectangular block inside water has a depth of immersion of 2 m from its base. If it is 4 m wide,20 m long and 5 m depth of uniform cross-section, what will be the metacentric height?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.33 m"},{"key":"B","html":"0.27 m"},{"key":"C","html":"0.67 m"},{"key":"D","html":"0.83 m"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f5/f599e4a3cfe820e8b2ce.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a laminar flow between two fixed parallel plates, the shear stress is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"constant across the passage"},{"key":"B","html":"maximum at centre and zero at the boundary"},{"key":"C","html":"zero all through the passage"},{"key":"D","html":"maximum at the boundary and zero at the centre"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9a/9a4a5010f5356ca4914f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Streamlining of blunt body shapes.<br />\n2. Fluid injection into the boundary layer.<br />\n3. Suction of fluid from the boundary layer.<br />\n4. Creating a motion of the boundary wall.<br />\nWhich of the above methods to control the boundary layer separation is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Oil of viscosity 2 poise and relative density 0.6 flow through a 20 cm diameter pipe. The head loss in 500 m length of pipe is 40 m. Assume laminar flow of oil in the pipe, and take density of water as 1000 kg/m<sup>3</sup>.</p>\n\n<p>What is the shear stress at the pipe wall?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"27.75 Pa"},{"key":"B","html":"34.44 Pa"},{"key":"C","html":"23.5 Pa"},{"key":"D","html":"20.57 Pa"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/71ea3ff0e226921c03bd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Oil of viscosity 2 poise and relative density 0.6 flow through a 20 cm diameter pipe. The head loss in 500 m length of pipe is 40 m. Assume laminar flow of oil in the pipe, and take density of water as 1000 kg/m<sup>3</sup>.</p>\n\n<p>What is the shear stress at a radial distance of 5 cm from the pipe axis?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"8.72 Pa"},{"key":"B","html":"15.7 Pa"},{"key":"C","html":"11.75 Pa"},{"key":"D","html":"19.62 Pa"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/31/31a48e3872256d98d646.png\" /></div>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Oil of viscosity 2 poise and relative density 0.6 flow through a 20 cm diameter pipe. The head loss in 500 m length of pipe is 40 m. Assume laminar flow of oil in the pipe, and take density of water as 1000 kg/m<sup>3</sup>.</p>\n\n<p>What is the value of friction factor in the pipe flow?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.087"},{"key":"B","html":"0.055"},{"key":"C","html":"0.036"},{"key":"D","html":"0.012"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/51c0a78bfaf7779c3152.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A discharge of 500 L/s of oil is passing through a 10 cm diameter pipe in smooth turbulent flow regime. The friction factor for this flow is estimated as 0.08. What will be the shear velocity in the pipe?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"63.66 m/s"},{"key":"B","html":"7.85 m/s"},{"key":"C","html":"6.36 m/s"},{"key":"D","html":"9.74 m/s"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fe/fe174230021fa4aa8eb0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A 50 cm diameter pipe conveys water through a 20 cm diameter nozzle for purposes of power generation. If the friction factor for pipe is 0.02, then the required length of pipe for the condition of maximum power is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"525.7 m"},{"key":"B","html":"990.5 m"},{"key":"C","html":"488.2 m"},{"key":"D","html":"1279.8 m"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/811177663abd319de232.png\" /></div>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> The buoyant force of a floating body passes through the centroid of the displaced volume.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> The force of buoyancy is vertical force and is equal to the weight of the fluid displaced.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A point <em>P</em>(3, 4, 5)<sup>T</sup> is attached to a rotating frame. The frame rotates 90° about the x-axis of the reference frame. What is the coordinates of the point relative to the reference frame after the rotation?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/ae/ae9d220441864464c0a3.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/32/3284aabfb1ab7b56db9d.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmdidle\"  src=\"media/21/21fbe62fce0989b45ff9.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/ed/ede9b67d335a074e606d.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/49/493d85ced5a667cdf5ee.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/08/0854864aad7c5145646e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A frame B was rotated about the y-axis 90°, then it was translated about the current a-axis 4 units of before it was rotated about the z-axis 90°. Finally, it was translated about current o-axis 8 units. Which one of the following equation represent <em><sup>U</sup>T<sub>B</sub></em>?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Rot (y,90°)Rot(z,90°)Trans(0,0,4)Trans(0,8,0)"},{"key":"B","html":"Rot (y,90°)Trans(0,0, 4)Rot(z,90°)Trans(0,8,0)"},{"key":"C","html":"Rot(z,90°)Trans(0,0, 4)Rot (y,90°)Trans(0,8,0)"},{"key":"D","html":"Rot(z,90°)Rot (y,90°)Trans(0,0,4)Trans(0,8,0)"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5f/5f6a130ab30743bddebe.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following joint symbol is used for Revolute joint in robots?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/fc/fcbc39b9769557bf00b1.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/09/093284573f2a81f5e8a3.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/40/4052dedaae5bf49a3c60.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/63/633f8fdbed7d9c38e54e.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b584034db4f07a982567.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> A robot programming language serves as an interface between the user and the robot manipulator.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> In on-line programming, the robot is not tied-up and can continue doing its task, that is, there is no loss of production.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIn on-line programming, the manipulator executes the command as soon as it is entered and the programmer can verify whether the robot executes the desired task. Any discrepancy is, therefore, corrected immediately. Statement II is correct for offline programming.</p>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following is the main advantage of air vessel when fitted on delivery side of reciprocating pump?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Reduces cavitation possibility"},{"key":"B","html":"The pump can be run at higher speed"},{"key":"C","html":"Suction pipe length can be increased"},{"key":"D","html":"Almost constant delivery discharge is obtained"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nAdvantage of air vessel on delivery side<br />\n(i) Almost constant delivery discharge is obtained.<br />\n(ii) Reduction in friction loss and hence saving in power.</p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A francis turbine develops 12 MW of power with a discharge of 10 m<sup>3</sup>/s of water. The difference of the piezometric heads and the kinetic heads between the entrance to and exit from the runner is 80 m and 50 m, respectively. The loss of head in the runner is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5.25 m"},{"key":"B","html":"3.48 m"},{"key":"C","html":"9.52 m"},{"key":"D","html":"7.67 m"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/10ae783a57dcb78955a6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A Kaplan turbine develops 20 MW power at a head of 40 m. The diameter of the boss is 0.4 times the diameter of the runner, if the overall efficiency of turbine is 0.9, the discharge through the turbine is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"72.58 m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"B","html":"44.72 m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"C","html":"56.63 m<sup>3</sup>/s"},{"key":"D","html":"67.67 m<sup>3</sup>/s"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b569d1016dcce5ff55c6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A pelton wheel installation which receives water from a reservoir through a penstock has a gross head at the wheel of 375 m. A nozzle supplies 5 m<sup>3</sup>/s of water to the turbine and the loss of head in the penstock could be taken as one third of the gross head. If the overall efficiency is 0.8, the power generated by the pelton wheel is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4.96 MW"},{"key":"B","html":"7.82 MW"},{"key":"C","html":"12.7 MW"},{"key":"D","html":"9.81 MW"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dc/dc3ef9db598145866bfd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A centrifugal pump has an impeller of outer diameter 30 cm rotating at a speed of 1500 rpm against a head of 20 m. The monometric efficiency is 0.9. Assuming radial flow at the inlet of pump, the whirl velocity at outlet is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"12.57 m/s"},{"key":"B","html":"8.58 m/s"},{"key":"C","html":"9.25 m/s"},{"key":"D","html":"13.82 m/s"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f4b2394673c62e0e7f8f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3b4a85b9b489ede0987e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":43,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a single acting reciprocating pump without air vessel, the maximum velocity in the suction pipe is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/5b/5bfe91ea3a3900ea1037.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/e7/e71817cde7c4811a0188.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/93/93a05033ac25be19fdb2.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/85/85680ab9034ea9f06ac9.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0e2c728dbdade60989df.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A turbojet power plant uses aviation kerosene having a calorific value of 45 MJ/kg. The fuel consumption is 0.2 kg per hour per N of thrust, when the thrust is 9 kN. The aircraft velocity is 500 m/s, the mass of air passing through the compressor is 30 kg/s.</p>\n\n<p>What is the air fuel ratio?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"30 : 1"},{"key":"B","html":"15 : 1"},{"key":"C","html":"45 : 1"},{"key":"D","html":"60 : 1"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/2372cc654a967b353e65.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A turbojet power plant uses aviation kerosene having a calorific value of 45 MJ/kg. The fuel consumption is 0.2 kg per hour per N of thrust, when the thrust is 9 kN. The aircraft velocity is 500 m/s, the mass of air passing through the compressor is 30 kg/s.</p>\n\n<p>What is the thrust power produced by turbojet?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1500 kW"},{"key":"B","html":"5000 kW"},{"key":"C","html":"4500 kW"},{"key":"D","html":"5500 kW"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5f/5fc490690b0793a2ffa4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A turbojet power plant uses aviation kerosene having a calorific value of 45 MJ/kg. The fuel consumption is 0.2 kg per hour per N of thrust, when the thrust is 9 kN. The aircraft velocity is 500 m/s, the mass of air passing through the compressor is 30 kg/s.</p>\n\n<p>What is the overall efficiency?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"10%"},{"key":"B","html":"20%"},{"key":"C","html":"40%"},{"key":"D","html":"30%"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c1871653548aee55d776.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A centrifugal compressor has to deliver 40 kg of air per sec. The impeller is 75 cm diameter with an adiabatic efficiency of 80%. Air enters the compressor at 1 bar and 27°C and compressed upto 4.2 bar.<br />\nThe radial velocity is 120 m/s and it is constant [Use 4.2<sup>0.287</sup> = 1.5]</p>\n\n<p>What is the temperature of air at the exit of compressor?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"450 K"},{"key":"B","html":"447.5 K"},{"key":"C","html":"477.8 K"},{"key":"D","html":"487.5 K"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aac9e39ff95477ea7747.png\" /></div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A centrifugal compressor has to deliver 40 kg of air per sec. The impeller is 75 cm diameter with an adiabatic efficiency of 80%. Air enters the compressor at 1 bar and 27°C and compressed upto 4.2 bar.<br />\nThe radial velocity is 120 m/s and it is constant [Use 4.2<sup>0.287</sup> = 1.5]</p>\n\n<p>What is the axial width of the impeller at the impeller tip?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"12 cm"},{"key":"B","html":"2.3 cm"},{"key":"C","html":"4.7 cm"},{"key":"D","html":"9.5 cm"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d0b30ca66f2314f3163.png\" /></div>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In an axial flow compressor, absolute velocity of air<br />\n1. Increases in rotar blades<br />\n2. Decreases in stator blades<br />\n3. Decreases in rotor blades<br />\n4. Increases in stator blade<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 3"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 4"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"3 and 4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> The operation of compressor on the left of the peak of the performance curve is injurious to the machine.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Surging of the compressor leads to vibration of the entire machine which ultimately lead to mechanical failure.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nSurge points on each curve corresponding to different speeds can be located and a surge line drawn. The stable range of operation of the compressor is on the right hand side of this line.</p>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following correctly defines the degree of reaction?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/4d/4d3fbacde02f5c17c4ba.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/4f/4f31672df7618530d9bf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>[where ‘Δ<em>h</em>’ is the change in enthalpy of working fluid]</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3b5216c9e0690f069b98.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a reaction stage turbine, the pressure</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"remains constant in both rotor and stator"},{"key":"B","html":"increases in stator and decreases in rotor"},{"key":"C","html":"increases in stator and remains constant"},{"key":"D","html":"decreases in stator and also in rotor"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nIn a reaction turbine pressure decreases in stator and also in rotor whereas in impulse turbine it<br />\nremains constant in both rotor and stator.</p>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A boiler uses 1800 kg/h of coal. The temperature of air supplied is 300 K and the average temperature of the flue gas leaving the chimney is 600 K. The 35 m high steel chimney produces a draught for the flow of gases. The quantity of air supplied is 20 kg/kg fuel and the density of flue gases is 0.6 kg/m<sup>3</sup>. Assume that 10% of the theoretical draught is used for creating the flow velocity of gases through the chimney.</p>\n\n<p>What is the amount of draught produced in terms of column of hot gases?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"42.5 m"},{"key":"B","html":"39.7 m"},{"key":"C","html":"31.6 m"},{"key":"D","html":"24.5 m"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/126f2ebddea2bf7f3daa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A boiler uses 1800 kg/h of coal. The temperature of air supplied is 300 K and the average temperature of the flue gas leaving the chimney is 600 K. The 35 m high steel chimney produces a draught for the flow of gases. The quantity of air supplied is 20 kg/kg fuel and the density of flue gases is 0.6 kg/m<sup>3</sup>. Assume that 10% of the theoretical draught is used for creating the flow velocity of gases through the chimney.</p>\n\n<p>What is the mass flow rate of the flue gases?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"11.5 kg/s"},{"key":"B","html":"10.5 kg/s"},{"key":"C","html":"7.5 kg/s"},{"key":"D","html":"12.5 kg/s"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c9/c9d2e5f8d5171e641f2f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A boiler uses 1800 kg/h of coal. The temperature of air supplied is 300 K and the average temperature of the flue gas leaving the chimney is 600 K. The 35 m high steel chimney produces a draught for the flow of gases. The quantity of air supplied is 20 kg/kg fuel and the density of flue gases is 0.6 kg/m<sup>3</sup>. Assume that 10% of the theoretical draught is used for creating the flow velocity of gases through the chimney.</p>\n\n<p>What is flue gas velocity through the chimney?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"15.7 m/s"},{"key":"B","html":"4.55 m/s"},{"key":"C","html":"11.7 m/s"},{"key":"D","html":"7.87 m/s"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e7fa35f6a864e2c79c4c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":56,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A boiler uses 1800 kg/h of coal. The temperature of air supplied is 300 K and the average temperature of the flue gas leaving the chimney is 600 K. The 35 m high steel chimney produces a draught for the flow of gases. The quantity of air supplied is 20 kg/kg fuel and the density of flue gases is 0.6 kg/m<sup>3</sup>. Assume that 10% of the theoretical draught is used for creating the flow velocity of gases through the chimney.</p>\n\n<p>What is the base diameter of the chimney?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3.5 m"},{"key":"B","html":"1.7 m"},{"key":"C","html":"5.6 m"},{"key":"D","html":"4.2 m"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/34045334ddabc421af34.png\" /></div>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding fire tube boilers:<br />\n1. Low first cast<br />\n2. Maximum operating pressure is equal or less than 18 bar<br />\n3. Less draught required<br />\n4. Quick response to load changes<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 4 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Steam is supplied to a turbine at 100 bar and 470°C. The steam while in expansion is taken out at 12 bar, 210°C for reheating and comes out from heater at 11 bar and 470°C and expands to 0.07 bar. The steam rate is 4 kg/kWh and the generator efficiency is 0.9. The heat loss through the turbine casing is 3% of the throttle enthalpy.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/59/59993ec5b7ed126017bf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>From Mollier chart : Refer figure<br />\n<em>h</em><sub>1</sub> = 3300 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>2</sub> = 2766 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>2</sub>′ = 2842 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>3</sub> = 3417 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>4</sub> = 2367 kJ/kg</div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What is the amount of net work produced by the turbine?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1200 kJ/kg"},{"key":"B","html":"1400 kJ/kg"},{"key":"C","html":"1800 kJ/kg"},{"key":"D","html":"1000 kJ/kg"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/de7a352cbd7c63f07628.png\" /></div>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Steam is supplied to a turbine at 100 bar and 470°C. The steam while in expansion is taken out at 12 bar, 210°C for reheating and comes out from heater at 11 bar and 470°C and expands to 0.07 bar. The steam rate is 4 kg/kWh and the generator efficiency is 0.9. The heat loss through the turbine casing is 3% of the throttle enthalpy.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/59/59993ec5b7ed126017bf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>From Mollier chart : Refer figure<br />\n<em>h</em><sub>1</sub> = 3300 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>2</sub> = 2766 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>2</sub>′ = 2842 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>3</sub> = 3417 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>4</sub> = 2367 kJ/kg</div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What is the enthalpy of steam after exit from the turbine?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1800 kJ/kg"},{"key":"B","html":"1944 kJ/kg"},{"key":"C","html":"2776 kJ/kg"},{"key":"D","html":"1567 kJ/kg"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/58/58e8bedcd0682d5f6463.png\" /></div>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Steam is supplied to a turbine at 100 bar and 470°C. The steam while in expansion is taken out at 12 bar, 210°C for reheating and comes out from heater at 11 bar and 470°C and expands to 0.07 bar. The steam rate is 4 kg/kWh and the generator efficiency is 0.9. The heat loss through the turbine casing is 3% of the throttle enthalpy.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/59/59993ec5b7ed126017bf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>From Mollier chart : Refer figure<br />\n<em>h</em><sub>1</sub> = 3300 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>2</sub> = 2766 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>2</sub>′ = 2842 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>3</sub> = 3417 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>4</sub> = 2367 kJ/kg</div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What is the internal efficiency of turbine upto extraction point?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"77.54%"},{"key":"B","html":"85.76%"},{"key":"C","html":"91.74%"},{"key":"D","html":"72.45%"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b3d36bba5963513bca12.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Steam is supplied to a turbine at 100 bar and 470°C. The steam while in expansion is taken out at 12 bar, 210°C for reheating and comes out from heater at 11 bar and 470°C and expands to 0.07 bar. The steam rate is 4 kg/kWh and the generator efficiency is 0.9. The heat loss through the turbine casing is 3% of the throttle enthalpy.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/59/59993ec5b7ed126017bf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>From Mollier chart : Refer figure<br />\n<em>h</em><sub>1</sub> = 3300 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>2</sub> = 2766 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>2</sub>′ = 2842 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>3</sub> = 3417 kJ/kg, <em>h</em><sub>4</sub> = 2367 kJ/kg</div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What is the overall efficiency of the turbine?</div>\n\n<p> </p>\n\n<p> </p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"78.25%"},{"key":"B","html":"82.41%"},{"key":"C","html":"72.77%"},{"key":"D","html":"63.13%"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b63da8fa1f5c24cdbeba.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding draught produced in a boiler:<br />\n1. Rate at which combustion takes place.<br />\n2. Characteristics of fuel and its depth on the grate.<br />\n3. Design of combustion chamber and the method of burning the fuel.<br />\n4. Resistance in flue gases circuits offered by baffles, tubes superheater etc.<br />\nWhich of the above factors, controls the draught produced in a boiler?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 4 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following is a water tube boiler?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Cochran boiler"},{"key":"B","html":"Lancashire boiler"},{"key":"C","html":"Cornish boiler"},{"key":"D","html":"Stirling boiler"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nThe examples of the water tube boiler are Bobcock and Wilcox boiler, stirling boiler, power boiler for utility etc.</p>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a boiler employing natural circulation, the circulation ratio is 10. The density of saturated water in the downcomer is 700 kg/m<sup>3</sup> and the density of steam-water mixture at the top of riser is 320 kg/m<sup>3</sup>. The height of riser wall is 30 m.</p>\n\n<p>What is the quality of steam at the top of riser?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.05"},{"key":"B","html":"0.2"},{"key":"C","html":"0.4"},{"key":"D","html":"0.1"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8d/8d15836e58f2aa374925.png\" /></div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a boiler employing natural circulation, the circulation ratio is 10. The density of saturated water in the downcomer is 700 kg/m<sup>3</sup> and the density of steam-water mixture at the top of riser is 320 kg/m<sup>3</sup>. The height of riser wall is 30 m.</p>\n\n<p>What is the pressure head developed due to natural circulation?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"105.7 kPa"},{"key":"B","html":"87.5 kPa"},{"key":"C","html":"93.2 kPa"},{"key":"D","html":"98.4 kPa"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/47/4770ea7474c375c747f2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":66,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In an impulse turbine, the change in whirl component of velocity is 400 m/s whereas the flow velocities at inlet and exit of turbine runner are 150 m/s and 100 m/s respectively, if the steam flow rate is 10 kg/s, the resultant thrust on the blades is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4125 N"},{"key":"B","html":"4095 N"},{"key":"C","html":"4225 N"},{"key":"D","html":"4031 N"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9dde35a2e12db2a5df12.png\" /></div>"},{"n":67,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following is an external loss in steam turbines?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Regulating valve losses"},{"key":"B","html":"Nozzle or fixed blade losses"},{"key":"C","html":"Carry over losses"},{"key":"D","html":"Leakage losses from the end seals"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nExternal losses are those which do not influence the steam conditions. They are<br />\n(i) Mechanical losses and<br />\n(ii) Leakage losses from the end seals.</p>"},{"n":68,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A four-stroke gas engine has a bore of 20 cm and stroke of 35 cm and runs at 400 rpm firing every cycle. If air fuel ratio is 4 : 1 by volume and volumetric efficiency on NTP basis is 80%, calorific value of the gas is 8 MJ/m<sup>3</sup> at NTP and the brake thermal efficiency is 25%, the brake power of the engine is <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/6b/6b5d079e5d31df3154f9.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"54.17 kW"},{"key":"B","html":"14.67 kW"},{"key":"C","html":"18.33 kW"},{"key":"D","html":"73.33 kW"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d76d250b15869081785.png\" /></div>"},{"n":69,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following factors affect the volumetric efficiency of the engine?<br />\n1. Density of fresh charge.<br />\n2. Exhaust gas in clearance volume.<br />\n3. Design of intake and exhaust manifolds.<br />\n4. Timing of the intake and exhaust valves.<br />\nSelect the correct answer using the codes given below:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":70,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding exhaust emissions:<br />\n1. The main non-exhaust is the unburnt hydrocarbons from fuel tank and crankcase blowby.<br />\n2. CO emissions will be very high at all equivalence ratio.<br />\n3. NO<sub>x</sub> emission will steadily increase as equivalence ratio increase.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; CO emissions will be very low at all equivalence ratio since excess air is always available.<br />\n&bull; NOx<br />\nemission will steadily increase as equivalence ratio increase due to increasing fraction of<br />\ncylinder contents due to increasing fraction of cylinder contents being burnt gases close to<br />\nstoichiometric during combustion, and also due to higher peak temperatures and pressures.</p>"},{"n":71,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Fuel-air cycle analysis suggests that the thermal efficiency will deteriorate as the mixture supplied to an engine is enriched.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Losses due to variable specific heats and dissociation result in decrease of thermal efficiency.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nFuel-air cycle analysis suggests that the thermal efficiency will deteriorate as the mixture supplied<br />\nto an engine is enriched. This is explained by the increasing losses due to variable specific heats<br />\nand dissociation as the mixture strength approaches chemically correct values. This is because,<br />\nthe gas temperature goes up after combustion as the mixture strength approaches chemically<br />\ncorrect values. Enrichment beyond the chemically correct ratio will lead to incomplete combustion<br />\nand loss in thermal efficiency. Therefore, it will appear that thermal efficiency will increase as the<br />\nmixture is made leaner.</p>"},{"n":72,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In an engine working on an ideal Otto cycle, the temperatures at the beginning and at the end of compression are 27°C and 327°C. The air standard efficiency of the engine is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"50%"},{"key":"B","html":"62%"},{"key":"C","html":"54%"},{"key":"D","html":"69%"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b5193d85855b98e57017.png\" /></div>"},{"n":73,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The actual operating pressure inside the cylinder of a spark ignition engine is below atmospheric</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"during intake and exhaust"},{"key":"B","html":"during intake"},{"key":"C","html":"during exhaust"},{"key":"D","html":"never during the cycle"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4d9b8de8f42a4f66845f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":74,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding battery ignition system:<br />\n1. The primary winding consists of many turns of fine copper wire while the secondary winding consists of few turns of heavy copper wire.<br />\n2. The primary voltage decreases as the engine speed increases.<br />\n3. The rapid collapse and reversal of the magnetic field in the core induce a very high voltage in the secondary winding.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; The ignition coil of two coils of wire, one wound around the other, insulated from each other;<br />\nthe primary winding consists of few turns of heavy copper wire and the secondary winding<br />\nconsists of many turns of fine copper wire.<br />\n&bull; Statement 2 is correct and it occurs due to limitations in the current switching capability of the<br />\nbreaker system.</p>"},{"n":75,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding stages of combustion in an SI engine:<br />\n1. The first stage of combustion is a chemical process and involves self propagation of nucleus of flame.<br />\n2. During second stage, the flame propagates practically at a constant velocity.<br />\n3. The starting point of the third stage is usually the instant at which the maximum pressure is reached.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":76,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A platform is suspended by two cables which are connected to blocks of weights 5 kN and 10 kN resting on horizontal surfaces as shown in figure below. The value of <em>W</em> at which the platform will descend</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/35/3586673c610272de66d8.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1.5 kN"},{"key":"B","html":"3 kN"},{"key":"C","html":"1 kN"},{"key":"D","html":"2 kN"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3d4728f4cf9a6e31fb18.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d4/d4470e9fc5b703c6777d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":77,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The truss <em>ABCD</em> is connected with a cable <em>BE</em> as shown. What is the force in member <em>AB</em>?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/ae/ae436c5e4b709916e209.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/dd/dd9720230c5b8a137602.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>P</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"2<em>P</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/26/2642ede5022fff2598c3.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/54/54a5d731fa35e78c7678.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b0/b0080df9e61100886725.png\" /></div>"},{"n":78,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A man of mass 75 kg is standing on the floor of an elevator. The elevator starts from rest and travels upward with a velocity which varies according to time following the law, <em>v</em> = (3t + 0.25t<sup>2</sup>) m/s. What upward force will the elevator floor exert on the man at <em>t</em> = 4 sec.?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"375 N"},{"key":"B","html":"750 N"},{"key":"C","html":"1125 N"},{"key":"D","html":"1950 N"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/28/28aebe1bac8cc758939a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":79,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A cold storage plant is required to store 200 kg of fish. The fish is supplied at a temperature of 32°C. The specific heat of fish above freezing point is 3 kJ/kgK. The specific heat of fish below freezing point is 1.2 kJ/kgK. The fish is stored in cold storage which is maintained at –8°C. The freezing point of fish is –4°C. The latent heat of fish is 230 kJ/kg. The plant requires 75 kW. The operating COP of the plant is 2.</p>\n\n<p>The capacity of the plant is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"21.4 TR"},{"key":"B","html":"142 TR"},{"key":"C","html":"42.8 TR"},{"key":"D","html":"71 TR"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7f/7fce18b2e02e0566f09d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":80,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A cold storage plant is required to store 200 kg of fish. The fish is supplied at a temperature of 32°C. The specific heat of fish above freezing point is 3 kJ/kgK. The specific heat of fish below freezing point is 1.2 kJ/kgK. The fish is stored in cold storage which is maintained at –8°C. The freezing point of fish is –4°C. The latent heat of fish is 230 kJ/kg. The plant requires 75 kW. The operating COP of the plant is 2.</p>\n\n<p>The time taken to achieve cooling is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6.5 min"},{"key":"B","html":"2.5 min"},{"key":"C","html":"7.6 min"},{"key":"D","html":"5.2 min"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/850fa735b4a8c3297d25.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dc/dc5650249d1540b09f8e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":81,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding refrigerants:<br />\n1. Refrigerants that are not miscible with oil pose problems in case of hermetically sealed compressors.<br />\n2. The phenomenon of moisture choking occurs in compressor.<br />\n3. In halide torch method of leak detection, the flame colour changes from light blue to bluish green in presence of ammonia.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are INCORRECT?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nIf more water is present than can be dissolved by the refrigerant, then there is danger of ice<br />\nformation and consequent choking in the expansion valve or capillary tube used for throttling.<br />\nThis phenomenon is called moisture choking.</p>"},{"n":82,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following are influenced or changed when a flash chamber is used in a vapour compression refrigeration system?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"COP"},{"key":"B","html":"Power input"},{"key":"C","html":"Size of evaporator"},{"key":"D","html":"Refrigeration effect"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nA flash chamber is an insulated container and it separates the liquid and vapour refrigerant coming out of expansion valve due to centrifugal effect. Due to this, the mass of the refrigerant passing through the evaporator reduces. This results in reduction in the mass of refrigerant flowing through the evaporator and hence the reduction in the size of evaporator.</p>"},{"n":83,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Atmospheric air at dry bulb temperature of 10°C enters a heating coil whose surface temperature is maintained at 50°C. The air at the exit of the coil is at 34°C dbt. The efficiency of the coil is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"40%"},{"key":"B","html":"60%"},{"key":"C","html":"66.67%"},{"key":"D","html":"33.33%"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f9d58777653712c0d4b3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":84,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> The performance of a centrifugal compressor is generally expressed in terms of adiabatic efficiency rather than isothermal efficiency.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> The minimum work of compression, with cooling, is isothermal work.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; The minimum work of compression, with cooling, is isothermal work. Often isothermal efficiency is, therefore, used to express the performance of reciprocating compressors which are invariably cooled.<br />\n&bull; The minimum of compression, without cooling, is isentropic work. Adiabatic efficiency is,<br />\ntherefore, used to express the performance of centrifugal compressors in which it is not possible to arrange cooling during compression.</p>"},{"n":85,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding the use of expansion devices in a refrigeration system:<br />\n1. Automatic expansion valves find applications where precise control of evaporator temperature is needed.<br />\n2. Thermostatic expansion valves keep the evaporator always full of the refrigerant.<br />\n3. An external-equilizer connection helps in transmitting the pressure at the outlet of evaporator to inside of the bellows.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n&bull; An automatic-expansion valve is not suitable for varying-load requirement. It is, however,<br />\nused in applications such as milk coolers where precise control of the evaporator temperature<br />\nis needed and where the cooling load is more or less constant.<br />\n&bull; A thermostatic expansion valve keeps the evaporators always full of the refrigerant, regardless<br />\nof the changes in the cooling load, by maintaining a constant degree of superheat of the<br />\nsuction vapour.<br />\n&bull; An external-equalizer connection transmits the pressure at the outlet of the evaporator to the<br />\ninside of the bellows of the expansion valve. At the same time, there is no connection between<br />\nthe evaporator inlet and inside of the bellows. Thus, the pressure acting inside the bellows is<br />\nalways equal to the pressure at the evaporator outlet, irrespective of the extent of pressure<br />\ndrop in the evaporator tubing.</p>"},{"n":86,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding recovery, grain growth and recrystallization:<br />\n1. The driving force for recovery, grain growth and recrystallization is stored energy of cold work.<br />\n2. Grain growth occurs only after recovery and recrystallization.<br />\n3. At a particular temperatures, grain growth occurs slowly while recrystallization occurs at substantially high speeds.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are INCORRECT?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; The driving force for grain growth is reduction in grain boundary region while for recovery<br />\nand recrystallization, it is stored energy of cold work.<br />\n&bull; Grain growth follows complete crystallization if the material is left at elevated temperatures.<br />\nHowever, grain growth does not need to be preceded by recovery and recrystallization; it<br />\nmay occur in all polycrystalline materials.</p>"},{"n":87,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following directions is common to both (1 1 0) and <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/c6/c654f7f7d0696b379fe8.png\" /> planes?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/1d/1d72d73afc8fd9264e67.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/c8/c88c1fa4a19b5f27c467.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/85/8512a5fab321b86136d0.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/d3/d346ce1a1652f3dfa1ec.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/91e50f6b597ffa5dcc88.png\" /></div>"},{"n":88,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The equilibrium crystal form of iron at ambient temperature and pressure is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"FCC (α)"},{"key":"B","html":"BCC (α)"},{"key":"C","html":"FCC (γ)"},{"key":"D","html":"BCC (δ)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":89,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In corrosion of metals by a gas like oxygen, the surface layer or film grows with time</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"linearly"},{"key":"B","html":"parabolically"},{"key":"C","html":"logarithmically"},{"key":"D","html":"all of the above, depending upon the nature of film formed"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nThe surface layer or film grows with time in following fashions:<br />\n&bull; linear (in case of porous films)<br />\n&bull; parabolic (in case of non porous adherent films)<br />\n&bull; logarithmic<br />\nThese three modes are known as laws of oxidation.</p>"},{"n":90,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The figure below shows the creep curve of a material for different temperatures, T<sub>I</sub>, T<sub>II</sub> and T<sub>III</sub>.<br />\nWhich of the following relationships is correct regarding the temperature?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/5e/5e14c4592572ef68d3b3.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/9e/9edb9bf22f06bf09be91.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/f6/f6f47bc64bc9ac883fe5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/8f/8f5ace043d93a84d76b1.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/38/38ba9e21c80d6387b180.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":91,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding necking phenomenon:<br />\n1. At the starting point of necking, the slope of true stress-strain curve is equal to the true stress at that point.<br />\n2. At the onset of necking, the strain is equal to strain hardening exponent.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0b/0bd15c4ea3ee8c0928b2.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1c/1c355028ac474f48c8f4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":92,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the correct decreasing order of hardness among the microconstituents of iron-carbon alloys?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Cementite - Ferrite - Pearlite"},{"key":"B","html":"Cementite - Pearlite - Ferrite"},{"key":"C","html":"Ferrite - Pearlite - Cementite"},{"key":"D","html":"Pearlite - Ferrite - Cementite"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":93,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding fatigue failure:<br />\n1. A condition of plain strain is found when work thickness is much greater than crack length.<br />\n2. Case hardening can help in extending fatigue life of a component.<br />\n3. Fatigue occurs when the maximum stress level reaches yield strength.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nFatigue occurs when the maximum stress level may be considerably lower than the static tensile or yield strength.</p>"},{"n":94,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A glass-fiber reinforced polymer (GFRP) composite is found to have a Young’s modulus of 12 GPa and a density 1.2 g/cm<sup>3</sup>. The composite is composed of glass (modulus 75 GPa, tensile strength 117 MPa, density 2.5 g/cm<sup>3</sup>) and polyurethane (modulus and strength unknown, density 1.1 g/cm<sup>3</sup>).</p>\n\n<p>What is the volume fraction of glass in the composite?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/a6/a6bb0330f8a39ac3d685.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/70/70d36cd3c2dbfbcef383.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/b5/b54c03212c19a1b8c038.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/ee/eef66d9d16b38fdfb118.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1e/1e2704c37ec617c72262.png\" /></div>"},{"n":95,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A glass-fiber reinforced polymer (GFRP) composite is found to have a Young’s modulus of 12 GPa and a density 1.2 g/cm<sup>3</sup>. The composite is composed of glass (modulus 75 GPa, tensile strength 117 MPa, density 2.5 g/cm<sup>3</sup>) and polyurethane (modulus and strength unknown, density 1.1 g/cm<sup>3</sup>).</p>\n\n<p>If the fibres are aligned longitudinally then the modulus of polyurethane is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"11 GPa"},{"key":"B","html":"93 GPa"},{"key":"C","html":"7.2 GPa"},{"key":"D","html":"1 GPa"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/57a3a3d784cffc444c56.png\" /></div>"},{"n":96,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding ceramics:<br />\n1. Ceramic crystal structures are stable when anions surrounding a cation are all in contact with that cation.<br />\n2. Microcracks cause stress amplification in both tension and compression in ceramics.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nFor compressive stresses, there is no stress amplification associated with any existent flows. For this reason, brittle ceramics displace much higher strengths in compression than in tension.</p>"},{"n":97,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A multi-disc clutch has three discs on the driving shaft and two on the driven shaft. The inside and outside diameters of the contact surface are 120 mm and 240 mm respectively. The maximum pressure between the surface is limited to 0.1 N/mm<sup>2</sup>. The clutch transmits 25 kW at 1575 rpm.</p>\n\n<p>The number of pairs of contact surfaces is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5"},{"key":"B","html":"6"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b32069a701988b22ca91.png\" /></div>"},{"n":98,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A multi-disc clutch has three discs on the driving shaft and two on the driven shaft. The inside and outside diameters of the contact surface are 120 mm and 240 mm respectively. The maximum pressure between the surface is limited to 0.1 N/mm<sup>2</sup>. The clutch transmits 25 kW at 1575 rpm.</p>\n\n<p>Assuming uniform wear condition, the axial force on each friction surface is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2262 N"},{"key":"B","html":"4524 N"},{"key":"C","html":"3393 N"},{"key":"D","html":"6786 N"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6d9dcc0d36f343f9659c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":99,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A single row angular contact ball bearing is used for an axial flow compressor. The bearing is to carry a radial load of 2500 N and an axial or thrust load of 1500 N. The desired life is to be 125 × 10<sup>6</sup> revolutions. If the load is uniform and steady, service factor is 1.5, radial factor is 1,<br />\nrotational factor is 1 and thrust factor is 0, then the basic dynamic load rating of the bearing is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"11.25 kN"},{"key":"B","html":"12.50 kN"},{"key":"C","html":"18.75 kN"},{"key":"D","html":"7.5 kN"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3dfa9cc591e336b8e77d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":100,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A plate 50 mm wide and 12.5 mm thick is to be welded to another plate by means of parallel fillet welds. The plates are subjected to a load of 50 kN. Assuming allowable shear strength to be 56 MPa, the minimum length of the weld will be nearly</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"49 mm"},{"key":"B","html":"51 mm"},{"key":"C","html":"36 mm"},{"key":"D","html":"101 mm"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/afeededbf5d0350588e9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":101,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following changes in specimen affect the results of a rotating beam test?<br />\n1. Surface condition<br />\n2. Stress state<br />\n3. Temperature<br />\n4. Size<br />\nSelect the correct answer using codes given below:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nRotating beam test is a laboratory method used to determine endurance limit. The endurance<br />\nlimit of a mechanical or structural member deviates from the experimentally determined values.<br />\nThese deviations are due to :<br />\n&bull; Material : Composition, basis of failure, variability.<br />\n&bull; Manufacturing : Method, heat treatment, surface condition, stress concentration.<br />\n&bull; Environment : Corrosion, temperature, stress state, relaxation times.<br />\n&bull; Design : Size, shape, life, speed, etc.</p>"},{"n":102,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Riveted lap joints carry smaller loads as compared to riveted butt joint.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Plates overlap each other in case of riveted lap joints.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3e8cbf8d714b561030f4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":103,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A solid shaft is transmitting 1 MW at 210 rpm. If the maximum torque transmitted exceeds the mean torque by 10% and the maximum allowable shear stress is 112 MPa, the diameter of the shaft is nearly</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"180 mm"},{"key":"B","html":"150 mm"},{"key":"C","html":"125 mm"},{"key":"D","html":"130 mm"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/67/673040f70ce082abd586.png\" /></div>"},{"n":104,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a flat belt drive, how much of the maximum tension is absorbed as centrifugal tension during transmission of maximum power?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"One-fourth"},{"key":"B","html":"One-third"},{"key":"C","html":"Half"},{"key":"D","html":"Two-third"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":105,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding stress concentration:<br />\n1. The effect of stress concentration is prominent even in case of static loading for brittle materials.<br />\n2. A better design is the one in which there is a single long notch rather than multiple small notches.<br />\n3. Notch sensitivity increases as the ductility of materials increases.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are incorrect?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dc/dc51fc52dd1eabcc737a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":106,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding mechanical brakes:<br />\n1. The brake should be self-locking in general applications.<br />\n2. The use of double block or shoe brakes is preferred over single block or shoe brake.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; The brake should be self energizing and not self locking in general applications. The selflocking brake is used only in back-stop applications.<br />\n&bull; When a single block or shoe brake is applied to a rolling wheel, an additional load is thrown<br />\non shaft bearings due to heavy normal force and produces bending of the shaft. This drawback<br />\nis overcome by double block or shoe brakes.</p>"},{"n":107,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding energy interaction in a thermodynamic system:<br />\n1. Work is a higher grade of energy as compared to heat.<br />\n2. Kinetic and potential energies are considered to be organised form of energy.<br />\n3. Internal energy cannot be readily converted into work.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n&bull; The kinetic energy and potential energy are also called organized form of energy that can be readily converted into work.<br />\n&bull; Internal energy is due to random motions or disorganised motions of molecules. The internal<br />\nenergy cannot be readily converted into work.</p>"},{"n":108,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A supply pump for a power plant takes in saturated water at 0.01 MPa and increases its pressure to 10 MPa. The pump has an adiabatic efficiency of 0.9. The saturated properties at 0.01 MPa are T<sub>s</sub> = 45.8°C; <em>h</em> = 200 kJ/kg; s = 0.650 kJ/kgK. Assume changes in density of water to be negligible and ambient temperature is 27°C.</p>\n\n<p>Calculate the irreversibility associated with this process. Use data given in table below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/71/71d18880334c3d8e2d8d.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1.5 kJ/kg"},{"key":"B","html":"2.1 kJ/kg"},{"key":"C","html":"3 kJ/kg"},{"key":"D","html":"0.7 kJ/kg"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/8277475d3d68daca70c6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":109,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A supply pump for a power plant takes in saturated water at 0.01 MPa and increases its pressure to 10 MPa. The pump has an adiabatic efficiency of 0.9. The saturated properties at 0.01 MPa are T<sub>s</sub> = 45.8°C; <em>h</em> = 200 kJ/kg; s = 0.650 kJ/kgK. Assume changes in density of water to be negligible and ambient temperature is 27°C.</p>\n\n<p>The second law efficiency for the pump is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"90%"},{"key":"B","html":"73%"},{"key":"C","html":"85%"},{"key":"D","html":"81%"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5da154be7dbea4aa76bc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":110,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A 10 m<sup>3</sup> tank is being filled with steam at 800 kPa and 400°C. It enters the tank through a 10 cm diameter pipe. Determine the rate at which the density is varying when the velocity of the steam in the pipe is 20 m/s. Take specific volume of steam at 800 kPa and 400°C as 0.341 m<sup>3</sup>/kg.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.08 kg/m<sup>3</sup>s"},{"key":"B","html":"0.05 kg/m<sup>3</sup>s"},{"key":"C","html":"0.02 kg/m<sup>3</sup>s"},{"key":"D","html":"0.20 kg/m<sup>3</sup>s"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/852fe9153c4d02fa714a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/70/7023daaf7c34f7749493.png\" /></div>"},{"n":111,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> It takes longer to cook at higher altitude regions.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> The weather is generally colder at higher altitude regions.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe atmospheric pressure, and thus the boiling temperature of water, decreases with elevation.<br />\nTherefore, it takes longer to cook at higher altitudes than it does at sea level. For each 1000 m<br />\nincrease in elevation, the boiling temperature drops by a little over 3&deg;C.<br />\nNote that the atmospheric pressure at a location, and thus the boiling temperature, changes<br />\nslightly with the weather conditions. But the corresponding change in the boiling temperature is<br />\nno more than about 1&deg;C.</p>"},{"n":112,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Compared to saturated vapour, superheated vapour is characterized by<br />\n1. lower pressures at a given temperature.<br />\n2. higher temperatures at a given pressure.<br />\n3. higher specific volumes at a given pressure or temperature.<br />\nSelect the correct answer using the codes given below:1 and 2 only</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":113,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The feed in face milling for a width of 75 mm with a cutter of 150 mm diameter, having 15 inserts and rotating at 300 rpm with a feed rate of 0.3 m/min is nearly</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.3 mm/tooth"},{"key":"B","html":"1 mm/tooth"},{"key":"C","html":"0.067 mm/tooth"},{"key":"D","html":"0.02 mm/tooth"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2c/2c947c7190d6dd8700c9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":114,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For spot welding of 2 mm thick sheet with a current flow of 0.8 seconds, the heat generated is 2000 J. If the effective resistance is 100 μΩ, the current required is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3162A"},{"key":"B","html":"5000A"},{"key":"C","html":"2500A"},{"key":"D","html":"1000A"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d9/d9335e2d51925833ac97.png\" /></div>"},{"n":115,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding brazing and soldering:<br />\n1. The flow of filler material takes place by capillary action into the joint.<br />\n2. Thermal residual stresses are negligible in soldered and brazed joints.<br />\n3. The strength of brazed joint is greater than that of soldered joint.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":116,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The variation in load with ram displacement in direct extrusion can be described as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"initially sharp increase reaching a maximum followed by slow decrease reaching a minimum and finally a sharp increase."},{"key":"B","html":"initially sharp increase reaching a maximum followed by steady slow decrease."},{"key":"C","html":"initially a sharp increase reaching a maximum and then following a constant line."},{"key":"D","html":"continuously increasing trend."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":117,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following G code can be used for parabolic interpolation in CNC machines?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"G02"},{"key":"B","html":"G03"},{"key":"C","html":"G06"},{"key":"D","html":"G40"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nG02 : Circular interpolation (Clockwise)<br />\nG03 : Circular interpolation (Anticlockwise)<br />\nG40 : Cutter compensation (Cancels to zero)</p>"},{"n":118,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> During strain hardening, the material shows increased resistance to deformation.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> The material undergoes changes in its crystalline structure during strain hardening.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nAfter undergoing the large strains that occur during yielding, the material begins to strain harden.<br />\nDuring strain hardening, the material undergoes changes in its crystalline structure, resulting in<br />\nincreased resistance of the material to further deformation.</p>"},{"n":119,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For a given material, the Young’s modulus is 110 GPa and the modulus of rigidity is 40 GPa. A round bar of 40 mm diameter and 2.5 m long of this material is stretched by 2.5 mm. What will be the lateral contraction (in mm)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.375"},{"key":"B","html":"0.0375"},{"key":"C","html":"0.015"},{"key":"D","html":"0.9375"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/40/405126967a39d824e9e3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":120,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The pinned members as shown in figure below carry the loads <em>P</em> and 2<em>P</em>. All bars have cross-sectional area <em>A</em>.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/2c/2c86d532bb6d864de080.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The ratio of stress in member <em>AF</em> to that of <em>AB</em> is nearly</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.9"},{"key":"B","html":"–1.1"},{"key":"C","html":"1.1"},{"key":"D","html":"–0.9"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/db/dbe1303f7b4298e8cd7c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4c/4c101addd71417e43d47.png\" /></div>"},{"n":121,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The ratio of stress in member <em>GF</em> to that of member <em>AF</em> is nearly</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1.2"},{"key":"B","html":"1.6"},{"key":"C","html":"0.6"},{"key":"D","html":"0.2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/67/676f42b921111eaa258f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":122,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a cantilever beam loaded only by the couple of 300 Nm applied as shown in the figure below. The shape of SFD of this beam will be</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/08/089dc028071956142e30.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"rectangular"},{"key":"B","html":"triangular"},{"key":"C","html":"straight line"},{"key":"D","html":"parabolic"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c114a0bede9496d8e075.png\" /></div>"},{"n":123,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A steel bar of rectangular cross-section 40 mm × 50 mm and pinned at each end is subjected to axial compression. The least radius of gyration for this bar is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"11.5 mm"},{"key":"B","html":"14.4 mm"},{"key":"C","html":"23 mm"},{"key":"D","html":"28.8 mm"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e3da95ae449b02cc4f16.png\" /></div>"},{"n":124,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A 1 m rectangular beam is attached to a 2 m long circular shaft and loaded as shown in figure below. The state of stress at point <em>A</em> is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/ce/ce02faae5d7e407700c8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/f3/f32ea026abba6484675a.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/e7/e719345b6154570f41bf.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/97/97f851d4e0f584590b01.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/03/03ad1fd85eba0a36c20d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":125,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Two beams <em>A</em> and <em>B</em> of different cross-sections are shown in figure below. Which of the two beams will be more effective in resisting bending?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/61/61447e81327e4998aba7.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Beam A"},{"key":"B","html":"Beam B"},{"key":"C","html":"Both will be equally resistant to bending"},{"key":"D","html":"Cannot say"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f9d52df952532bab3a87.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>"},{"n":126,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A load <em>P</em> is supported at <em>B</em> by two rods of the same material and of the same cross-section of the area <em>A</em> as shown in figure below. The strain energy of the system is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/65/65d4992be41523867601.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/27/27a4ec73aa412128bd7b.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/c8/c8239274ed3ec9de1470.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/df/dfc5edb6797c789b75bc.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/a6/a63f18a1f3240915bfa1.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/42/428e3261a881ce6679b4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d3912f0481136da62a8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":127,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding a shaft subjected to torsion:<br />\n1. Shaft of ductile material fails along the surfaces forming a 45° angle with its longitudinal axis.<br />\n2. Shaft of brittle material fails along a plane perpendicular to its longitudinal axis.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are incorrect?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/80/80f8cccdb64223de8357.png\" /></div>"},{"n":128,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A cantilever beam 3 m long carries a uniformly distributed load over the entire length. If the slope at the free end is 1°, find the deflection at the free end.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2 cm"},{"key":"B","html":"4 cm"},{"key":"C","html":"5 cm"},{"key":"D","html":"8 cm"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/27816245c5c8dcba7977.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/694536b5caff1de07a30.png\" /></div>\n</div>"},{"n":129,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A weight of 270 kg is dropped on a closely coiled helical spring consisting of 16 coils. Find the height by which the weight is dropped before striking the spring so that the spring may be compressed by 32 cm. The coils have a mean radius of 12 cm and the diameter of the rod of the<br />\nspring is 3 cm. Take <em>C</em> = 0.2 × 106 kg/cm<sup>2</sup></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"32 cm"},{"key":"B","html":"5.4 cm"},{"key":"C","html":"34.7 cm"},{"key":"D","html":"2.7 cm"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1b/1bde7d9a8d098b14a074.png\" /></div>"},{"n":130,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A cube of side 50 mm is subjected to shear stress and a graph between shear stress and shear strain is plotted. It is observed that elastic limit shear stress is 250 MPa. If shear modulus of material is 80 kN/mm<sup>2</sup>. The proof resilience will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"50 Nmm"},{"key":"B","html":"40 Nmm"},{"key":"C","html":"50 Nm"},{"key":"D","html":"60 Nm"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/41/415a84b5748e36579f83.png\" /></div>"},{"n":131,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a triangular section, maximum shear stress is 18 MPa. The shear stress at neutral axis is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"9 MPa"},{"key":"B","html":"16 MPa"},{"key":"C","html":"12 MPa"},{"key":"D","html":"24 MPa"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/501976c7b0c0491eda38.png\" /></div>"},{"n":132,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A company has a fixed costs of Rs.<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/61/61909b7f566e3850e09d.png\" /> and produces one product with a selling price of Rs.<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/2f/2f55f9a742f55ce28a94.png\" /> and a variable cost of Rs.<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/51/51690dc3e83fdaf355ec.png\" /> per unit. The maximum factory capacity is 20000 units and it anticipates selling 15000 units.</p>\n\n<p>The break even chart will show an intersection point at</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(4000, 300000)"},{"key":"B","html":"(10000, 420000)"},{"key":"C","html":"(10000, 720000)"},{"key":"D","html":"(4000, 720000)"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fb7d6b974d61a695619b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":133,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A company has a fixed costs of Rs.<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/61/61909b7f566e3850e09d.png\" /> and produces one product with a selling price of Rs.<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/2f/2f55f9a742f55ce28a94.png\" /> and a variable cost of Rs.<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/51/51690dc3e83fdaf355ec.png\" /> per unit. The maximum factory capacity is 20000 units and it anticipates selling 15000 units.</p>\n\n<p>What is the margin of sales at present?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/9d/9d123d52d24c62ea3eaa.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/50/50b596fd533348f8d1b7.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/1f/1f41cc545f1764395877.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/b8/b828d1f77790928da974.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/72/726372a14d6b7a01968b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":134,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A small project consists of seven activities for which the relevant data are given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/5d/5d7b2458f9ad14c3bd94.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following activities will have zero free float?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>B</em>, <em>D</em> and <em>G</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>C</em>, <em>E</em> and <em>F</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>B</em>, <em>E</em> and <em>F</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>A</em>, <em>D</em> and <em>G</em>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/828ce423dfb5b1e46029.png\" /></div>"},{"n":135,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The counter of a bank branch performs the transactions with a mean time of 2 minutes. The customers arrive at a mean rate of 20 customers/hour. If arrivals follow a Poisson process and that the service time is exponential, then the mean waiting time of the customers are</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4 minutes"},{"key":"B","html":"6 minutes"},{"key":"C","html":"3 minutes"},{"key":"D","html":"1.5 minutes"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/43/43b41ce4a810929a9c9e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/5787b64583775978be92.png\" /></div>"},{"n":136,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following can be categorized as a procurement cost?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Cost of insurance"},{"key":"B","html":"Cost of order preparation"},{"key":"C","html":"Taxes"},{"key":"D","html":"Cost of loss of goodwill"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nProcurement costs : This includes the cost of order preparation, tender placement, cost of postages, telephone costs, receiving costs, set up cost, etc.<br />\nCarrying costs : This represents the cost of maintaining inventories in the plant. It includes the cost of insurance, security, warehouse rent, taxes, interest on capital engaged, spoilage, breakage, etc.<br />\nStockout costs : This represents the cost of loss of demand due to shortage in supplies. This<br />\nincludes cost of loss of profit, loss of customer, loss of goodwill, penalty, etc.</p>"},{"n":137,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the maximum edge dimension of a solid aluminium cube at 100°C subjected to a convective heat transfer with <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/7b/7b3481bf6afedfe1a13f.png\" /> for a lumped analysis to be accurate to within 5%? [Assume thermal conductivity for aluminium is 225 W/mK]</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2.7 m"},{"key":"B","html":"5.4 m"},{"key":"C","html":"0.9 m"},{"key":"D","html":"1.8 m"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ce/ce062488e81d3efe0774.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":138,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Air at 350 K and standard atmospheric pressure (ν = 24 × 10<sup>–6</sup> m<sup>2</sup>/s) flows along a smooth flat plate at 12 m/s. For laminar flow, at what length from the leading edge does the boundary layer thickness reach 0.5 cm?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2 m"},{"key":"B","html":"5 m"},{"key":"C","html":"0.2 m"},{"key":"D","html":"0.5 m"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ed/ed8622688d32e3e9c1ad.png\" /></div>"},{"n":139,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Hot oil is used to heat water flowing at the rate of 0.1 kg/s from 40°C to 80°C in a counterflow double pipe heat exchanger. For an overall heat transfer coefficient of 300 W/m<sup>2</sup>K, find the heat transfer area if the oil enters at 105°C and leaves at 70°C.<br />\n[Take C<sub>p</sub> for water as 4 kJ/kgK; ln1.2 = 0.2; ln6.5 = 2]</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1.9 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"2.1 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"1.7 m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"1.6 m<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/17/17eeaba1d7ad41d86dce.png\" /></div>"},{"n":140,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Two long parallel surfaces, each of emissivity 0.6 are maintained at different temperatures and accordingly have radiation exchange between them. It is desired to reduce to 300 W from 400 W of radiant heat transfer by inserting thin parallel shields of equal emissivity 0.7 on both sides.<br />\nWhat would be the number of shields?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"3"},{"key":"D","html":"4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/69d29491f3d438a307af.png\" /></div>"},{"n":141,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding insulation of pipes and electrical wires :<br />\n1. Thickness of insulation for pipes is taken higher than critical thickness.<br />\n2. Thickness of insulation for wires is kept lower than critical thickness.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Statement 1 is correct. This is so because any insulation added will only decrease the heat loss from the pipe.<br />\n&bull; Statement 2 is correct. This is so that the insulation added increases the heat loss from the electrical wires.</p>"},{"n":142,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. The revolute (<em>R</em>) and the prismatic (<em>P</em>) pairs are the only lower pairs usable in a planar mechanism.<br />\n2. The slider joint is preferred over pin joint for low wear and long life.<br />\n3. A ball-and-socket joint is a 3 DOF joint.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; The revolute (R) and the prismatic (P) pairs are the only lower pairs usable in a planar mechanism. The screw (H), cylindric (C), spherical (S) and flat (F) lower pairs are all combinations of the revolute and/or prismatic pairs and are used in spatial (3D) mechanisms.<br />\n&bull; The main practical advantage of lower pairs over higher pairs is their better ability to trap lubricant between their enveloping surfaces. The lubricant is more easily squeezed out of a higher pair, non enveloping joint. As a result, the pin joint is preferred for low wear and long life over slider joint.</p>"},{"n":143,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding steering mechanisms:<br />\n1. In Davis type steering, the gear mechanism is placed on the back of the front wheel axle.<br />\n2. The Ackerman steering gear consists of turning pairs, whereas Davis steering gears consists of sliding pairs.<br />\n3. Davis mechanism is a complete and perfect mechanism.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":144,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For a horizontal single slider crank engine piston effort is 150 kN. Angle made by crank with line of stroke is 30° and angle made by connecting rod to the line of stroke is 45°, then crank effort for this mechanism will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"153.691 kN"},{"key":"B","html":"167.303 kN"},{"key":"C","html":"178.513 kN"},{"key":"D","html":"204.903 kN"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0f/0f642809d2665c6ad49d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":145,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the system <em>A</em> shown below where x is the downward displacement of the block, measured from the system’s equilibrium position which is used as the generalized coordinate. The system <em>B</em> is the equivalent linear system for system <em>A</em>.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/14/14f3d2f09f11d939de09.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>What is the value of <em>k<sub>eq</sub></em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2<em>k</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"3<em>k</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/62/6257abe4e2f85d35fc0d.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/17/1738e13afd139b8a850f.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73fe383425d804077562.png\" /></div>"},{"n":146,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the system <em>A</em> shown below where x is the downward displacement of the block, measured from the system’s equilibrium position which is used as the generalized coordinate. The system <em>B</em> is the equivalent linear system for system <em>A</em>.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/14/14f3d2f09f11d939de09.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>What is the value of <em>m<sub>eq</sub></em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/09/0973e1578d2c45c1a37d.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/13/1319409995400229bee7.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/7d/7d4bab597f745296eb59.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/f4/f4c5bb1f1d14c5eb4195.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0a/0a90330e32be89dd406d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":147,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding gears and gear trains:<br />\n1. The mechanical advantage of a gearset is the reciprocal of velocity ratio.<br />\n2. With an involute tooth form, centre distance errors do not affect the velocity ratio.<br />\n3. Backlash is not a problem in a gearset which runs with a non-reversing torque.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1 and 3 only"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n&bull; The torque ratio is the reciprocal of the velocity ratio; thus a gearset is essentially a device to exchange torque for velocity or vice versa. Since there are no applied forces as, in a linkage, but only applied torque on the gears, the mechanical advantage of a gearset is equal to its torque ratio.<br />\n&bull; Backlash is defined as the clearance between mating teeth measured along the circumference of the pitch circle. As long as the gearset is run with a nonreversing torque, backlash should not be a problem. But, whenever torque changes sign, the teeth will move from contact on one side to the other.</p>"},{"n":148,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>How many degree of freedom does the following mechanism have?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/cd/cd0e6269a6af232d1142.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"3"},{"key":"D","html":"4"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/8131d0240fdb4c27eb47.png\" /></div>"},{"n":149,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The mechanism show below can be categorised as</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/94/949326955f7280aff8ce.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Crank-rocker mechanism"},{"key":"B","html":"Double crank mechanism"},{"key":"C","html":"Double rocker mechanism"},{"key":"D","html":"Cannot say"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/010dd597a332f060e6aa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":150,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The figure shows a compound gear train. The input gear <em>A</em> rotates with a speed of 1200 rpm. The rpm of gear <em>G</em> is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/31/310a3c43759c4e4280ee.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"20 rpm (CCW)"},{"key":"B","html":"6857 rpm (CCW)"},{"key":"C","html":"6857 rpm (CW)"},{"key":"D","html":"210 rpm (CW)"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/632fd8a8c4f0092a6df0.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":150}],"stats":{"questions":150,"maxMarks":300,"types":{"MCQ":150},"withoutKey":0}};
